---

I basically need to be able to drop unpolished things. If I treat the blog as a set of polished essays, I will feel immense pressure with each of them.

If I'm allowed to just dump stuff but the layout is so that the interesting things are still highlighted (maybe it triages photos to photos, longreads to longreads, but you can choose to read just historically), I can then not be intimidated and just share. Some of my posts are «hey folks, how are you» and some are interesting and liked, and everyone is ok with that.

The unit is the fragment, not the article. A screenshot off the iPad, a photo of a drawn page, three lines, a link, once in a while a longread: each is a post the moment it is dropped in, with a timestamp and nothing else required. Nothing is ready or not ready. The archive is the content.

The lineage is the 2005 tumblelog (typed posts decided by what you dropped, one chronological stream, type filters) and Are.na (blocks of equal weight in a grid, channels over them). I take the typing from the first and the grid from the second.
